---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const spaceCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'width', label: 'room width', min: 200, max: 600, step: 10, value: 360, unit: 'cm' },
    { name: 'depth', label: 'room depth', min: 200, max: 600, step: 10, value: 420, unit: 'cm' },
    { name: 'table', label: 'table diameter', min: 80, max: 160, step: 5, value: 110, unit: 'cm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'clearance per side (walk space)', expr: '(width - table)/2', unit: 'cm' },
    { label: 'chairs fit? (60cm each)', expr: 'floor(3.14*(table/2+30)/60)', unit: ' seats' },
  ],
};

const fundamentalsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Elements and principles', note: 'Line, form, colour, texture, light — organised by balance, scale, rhythm, emphasis, harmony. The grammar every interior speaks.' },
    { math: '2 · Programming before drawing', note: 'Who uses the space, doing what, when? The brief (programme) drives the plan; aesthetics follow function here with rare exceptions.' },
    { math: '3 · Space planning is circulation', note: 'Rooms are flows: entry, paths, clearances, furniture zones. A beautiful room you cannot walk through is a failed plan.' },
    { math: '4 · Human dimensions are law-adjacent', note: 'Anthropometrics — reach, seat heights, clear widths — plus accessibility codes set the minimums no aesthetic may violate.' },
    { math: '5 · Light finishes the design', note: 'Daylight, ambient, task and accent lighting decide whether any of the above is even visible. Lighting is the final instrument.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/interior-design/foundational/interior-design-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Interior design is the shaping of interior space for human use — part art, part applied
    behavioural science, part code compliance. Its fundamentals run from the design elements and
    principles through programming (who does what here?) to space planning, where human
    dimensions and circulation decide whether the room actually works.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="elements, programme, circulation, dimensions, light" widget="equationStep" config={fundamentalsCfg} />

  <h2>Clearances are the quiet proof</h2>
  <p>
    Talk is cheap; clearances are checkable. A dining space needs roughly 90 cm of clear passage
    per side (60 cm squeezes); seats want about 60 cm of table edge each. Run the numbers on the
    room below and watch a "cosy" plan fail its own circulation. Professional space plans are
    drawn to the centimetre because bodies cannot negotiate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="dining clearances and seating capacity, computed" widget="sliderLab" config={spaceCfg} />

  <h2>Programming: the brief is the design</h2>
  <p>
    Before any sketch, CIDA-standard practice demands a programme: inventory the users, activities,
    frequencies, adjacencies and constraints. A family kitchen and a bakery's training kitchen
    share equipment lists and nothing else. Interiors designed without programming are styling —
    attractive, occasionally lucky, and unaccountable when they fail.
  </p>

  <h2>Universal design as the floor, not the ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Accessibility codes set minimum clear widths, turning spaces and reach ranges — but the
    discipline's better ambition is universal design: spaces usable by everyone without
    adaptation. Zero-step entries, lever handles, layered lighting that aids low vision. The
    elegant interior and the accessible interior were never opposites; that myth is what this
    profession exists to retire.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> CIDA Professional Standards; Panero &amp; Zelnik, <em>Human Dimension and Interior Space</em>; Nielson &amp; Taylor, <em>Interiors: An Introduction</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
